---
import SetupLayout from '../../layouts/SetupLayout.astro';
import SetupBrowserStarts from '../../components/SetupBrowserStarts.astro';
import { getActivePlatforms } from '../../lib/platforms';
const platforms = (await getActivePlatforms()).filter(p => p.data.tier === 'live');
---
<SetupLayout title="Get your program running." description="Start in the lesson. When you want your own editor and files, take the same work onto your computer.">
  <section id="browser">
    <h2>Start in your browser</h2>
    <p>These lessons let you edit, build and run a program here. Choose the one that interests you; BASIC and assembly are independent starting points.</p>
    <SetupBrowserStarts />
    <p>Other lessons may offer <strong>Run it here</strong> for a supplied program. That lets you try the result; editing and rebuilding it depends on the tools in that lesson.</p>
    <p>Still choosing what to learn? <a href="/start-here/">Start Here will help you find a route.</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="workspace">
    <h2>Work on your own computer</h2>
    <p>Choose your lesson’s machine and language. Each guide explains what you need, how to run an example and how to keep your changes.</p>
    <ul class="setup-choices">
      {platforms.map(platform => <li>
        <h3>{platform.data.name}</h3>
        <div class="setup-route-links">
          <a href={`/systems/${platform.id}/getting-started/`}>{['commodore-64', 'sinclair-zx-spectrum'].includes(platform.id) ? 'BASIC and assembly setup' : 'Assembly setup'}</a>
          {platform.id === 'commodore-amiga' && <><a href="/systems/commodore-amiga/amos/getting-started/">AMOS setup</a><a href="/systems/commodore-amiga/blitz/getting-started/">Blitz BASIC setup</a></>}
        </div>
      </li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section id="tools">
    <h2>A tool for each job</h2>
    <p>You only need the tools used by your chosen lesson. Browser lessons handle the steps they support within the page; local work gives you control of your editor, files and build commands.</p>
    <dl>
      <dt><strong><a href="https://asm198x.github.io/">Asm198x</a> · assemble source</strong></dt><dd><p>Turns assembly instructions into machine code. Use the dialect and output format given in the machine guide.</p></dd>
      <dt><strong><a href="https://build198x.github.io/">Build198x</a> · prepare files</strong></dt><dd><p>Tokenises Spectrum and C64 BASIC listings into loadable programs. It also prepares assets and media, including bootable Amiga disks.</p></dd>
      <dt><strong><a href="https://emu198x.github.io/">Emu198x</a> · run the machine</strong></dt><dd><p>Runs the program on an emulated computer or console. The lesson’s browser player or local guide sets out the machine and any firmware it needs.</p></dd>
    </dl>
    <p>Already use Homebrew? Asm198x, Build198x and Emu198x install on macOS (Apple silicon or Intel) and Linux (ARM64 or x86-64). The machine guides give you the commands. Emu198x has <a href="https://emu198x.github.io/downloads/">a separate package for each machine</a>; install only the ones you want. Windows downloads are available too.</p>
    <p>Some local emulators need the original machine’s built-in software. The <a href="/setup/roms/">ROM guide</a> explains which files you need and where to obtain them.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="check">
    <h2>Make it yours, then keep it</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Run the lesson’s example.</strong> Compare its screen and controls with the result shown in the lesson.</li>
      <li><strong>Change one thing.</strong> Make the suggested edit, rebuild if needed and check that your change appears.</li>
      <li><strong>Save the source.</strong> Download the edited listing or save it in your own project folder. Keep any assets it uses. A running emulator session is not a saved source file.</li>
      <li><strong>Open it again.</strong> Check that you can recover your work and run it before moving on.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If something goes wrong, keep the error message and check the machine, language and filenames against the guide. Installing an assembler does not change the language your source is written in.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="alternatives">
    <h2>Already using another assembler?</h2>
    <p>Asm198x is the recommended assembly path. These guides cover the reference tools if you already use them or want to compare their workflow.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/setup/commodore-64/native">C64 with ACME</a></li>
      <li><a href="/setup/sinclair-zx-spectrum/native">Spectrum with Pasmo</a></li>
      <li><a href="/setup/nintendo-entertainment-system/native">NES with ca65</a></li>
      <li><a href="/setup/commodore-amiga/native">Amiga with vasm</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section id="help">
    <h2>When a step does not work</h2>
    <p>Find the first result that differs from the guide. Check the filename, folder, tool version and machine configuration, and keep the exact error message.</p>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/code198x/website/issues/new">Report the page on GitHub</a> or <a href="https://discord.gg/AdmEYQKWZb">ask through Discord</a>. Include your operating system, language, tool versions and the step you reached.</p>
    <p>Preparing a class or club? The <a href="/teaching/">teaching guide</a> covers checking the computers and planning an activity.</p>
  </section>
</SetupLayout>
